escribe-images --repository-name payments-api --region ap-south-1\n\n### 4. Security and production considerations\n\nRegistry operations depend on IAM identity permissions and repository policies. Encryption configuration protects stored image data. Regional design, network endpoints, KMS permissions, and cross-account trust affect availability. Keep registry ownership and repository-level permissions clear.\n\nProduction implementation should be repeatable and reviewable. Keep infrastructure configuration and IAM policies in version control. Use short-lived credentials, protect sensitive command output, and separate development and production responsibilities. Record image provenance and verify the exact artifact that is promoted. Do not treat a successful command as sufficient evidence until the resulting AWS resource or image metadata has been checked.\n\n### 5. Troubleshooting and verification\n\nCheck the registry hostname, AWS account ID, Region, repository name, manifest availability, layer transfer, IAM permissions, repository policy, and network path. Distinguish a registry authentication failure from a repository authorization failure or a missing image manifest.\n\nVerify the outcome by checking the AWS CLI response, repository configuration, image metadata, tag, digest, and relevant CloudTrail activity. When a failure occurs, collect the exact error message, caller identity, requested action, resource ARN, account, Region, and timestamp. Change only the configuration responsible for the failure, then repeat the operation using the same identity and environment.\n\n### 6. DevOps pipeline application\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, this section should become an explicit and testable pipeline responsibility. The pipeline should validate its prerequisites, use an approved IAM role, execute the required operation, inspect the result, and stop when validation fails. Store the resulting image reference and digest with the build metadata. Pass verified artifact informa