        "id": "stage-9-topic-8-section-14",
        "title": "Image Pull Workflow — Hands-on Registry Exercise",
        "content": "## Image Pull Workflow — Hands-on Registry Exercise\n\n### 1. Technical explanation\n\nExplain the sequence of operations from the initiating client to the AWS service and the resulting registry state. Describe the components involved, the information exchanged, the dependencies between steps, and what should happen when an individual step fails.\n\nThe ECR pull workflow retrieves a container image from a repository into a Docker client or container runtime. The client resolves the image reference, requests the manifest, and downloads the layers needed to construct the image locally.\n\n### 2. How this works in practice\n\nConfirm the target account, Region, repository, and image reference. Authenticate using the identity configured for the environment, then pull the full repository URI and selected tag or digest. In ECS and EKS, ensure the configured execution role or node identity can retrieve the image.\n\nFor this section, the engineer should understand the relationship between the selected operation and the complete ECR image lifecycle. Confirm the target AWS account, Region, repository, and image reference before performing an operation. Use the identity intended for the actual pipeline or runtime instead of relying only on an administrator account. Record the relevant resource ARN, image tag, digest, and operation result so the work can be traced later.\n\n### 3. Practical AWS and Docker implementation\n\nThe following commands are relevant examples for this topic. Replace ACCOUNT_ID, repository names, image versions, role names, and Regions with values from the actual environment. Review commands before running any operation that changes AWS resources.\n\naws ecr get-login-password --region ap-south-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com\ndocker pull ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/payments-api:1.4.2\ndocker image inspect ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/payments-api:1.4.2\n\n### 4. Security and production considerations\n\nGrant pull permissions only to identities that require the artifact. Use digest references for controlled production deployments. Ensure network access to the registry and required service endpoints. Do not embed registry credentials in container images.\n\nProduction implementation should be repeatable and reviewable. Keep infrastructure configuration and IAM policies in version control. Use short-lived credentials, protect sensitive command output, and separate development and production responsibilities. Record image provenance and verify the exact artifact that is promoted. Do not treat a successful command as sufficient evidence until the resulting AWS resource or image metadata has been checked.\n\n### 5. Troubleshooting and verification\n\nFor image-pull errors, inspect the exact image URI, repository, tag, digest, authentication token, IAM permissions, network route, DNS, proxy, and runtime identity. Separate image retrieval problems from container startup, application configuration, and readiness failures.\n\nVerify the outcome by checking the AWS CLI response, repository configuration, image metadata, tag, digest, and relevant CloudTrail activity. When a failure occurs, collect the exact error message, caller identity, requested action, resource ARN, account, Region, and timestamp. Change only the configuration responsible for the failure, then repeat the operation using the same identity and environment.\n\n### 6. DevOps pipeline application\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, this section should become an explicit and testable pipeline responsibility. The pipeline should validate its prerequisites, use an approved IAM role, execute the required operation, inspect the result, and stop when validation fails. Store the resulting image reference and digest with the build metadata. Pass verified artifact information to the deployment stage so that downstream environments use the intended image rather than an unverified local build.\n\n### 7. Engineering checklist\n\n- Confirm the correct AWS account and Region.\n- Confirm the ECR repository and image reference.\n- Use the intended IAM identity and least-privilege permissions.\n- Verify the operation using AWS metadata or Docker inspection.\n- Record the image tag and digest where applicable.\n- Check security, retention, and rollback requirements.\n- Preserve diagnostic evidence and avoid exposing credentials."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Immutable Image Tags",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-9-topic-9-section-1",
        "title": "Immutable Image Tags — Registry Fundamentals and Architecture",
        "content": "## Immutable Image Tags — Registry Fundamentals and Architecture\n\n### 1. Technical explanation\n\nExplain the meaning, purpose, business value, main components, and where this capability fits into a container delivery platform. Clarify the difference between this concept and related ECR concepts so a beginner can understand when and why it is used.\n\nECR tag immutability prevents an existing image tag from being overwritten by a later push. It protects release references from silently changing to different image content and helps preserve traceability between a deployment and its original artifact.\n\n### 2. How this works in practice\n\nEnable immutable tag settings on the repository, publish each build with a unique release or commit identifier, and verify the image metadata after publication. When a tag already exists, create a new release identifier rather than reusing the protected tag.\n\nFor this section, the engineer should understand the relationship between the selected operation and the complete ECR image lifecycle. Confirm the target AWS account, Region, repository, and image reference before performing an operation. Use the identity intended for the actual pipeline or runtime instead of relying only on an administrator account. Record the relevant resource ARN, image tag, digest, and operation result so the work can be traced later.\n\n### 3. Practical AWS and Docker implementation\n\nThe following commands are relevant examples for this topic. Replace ACCOUNT_ID, repository names, image versions, role names, and Regions with values from the actual environment. Review commands before running any operation that changes AWS resources.\n\naws ecr describe-repositories --repository-names payments-api --region ap-south-1\naws ecr put-image-tag-mutability --repository-name payments-api --image-tag-mutability IMMUTABLE --region ap-south-1\naws ecr describe-images --repository-name payments-api --region ap-south-1\n\n### 4. Security and production considerations\n\nUse immutable tags for approved release artifacts and combine them with digest pinning, image scanning, and controlled promotion. Document any permitted exclusions or mutable-tag workflows. Limit repository configuration changes to authorized administrators.\n\nProduction implementation should be repeatable and reviewable. Keep infrastructure configuration and IAM policies in version control. Use short-lived credentials, protect sensitive command output, and separate development and production responsibilities. Record image provenance and verify the exact artifact that is promoted. Do not treat a successful command as sufficient evidence until the resulting AWS resource or image metadata has been checked.\n\n### 5. Troubleshooting and verification\n\nA push rejected because a tag already exists may indicate that immutability is working as designed. Check repository settings and the existing image digest. Do not delete an approved image simply to reuse its tag.\n\nVerify the outcome by checking the AWS CLI response, repository configuration, image metadata, tag, digest, and relevant CloudTrail activity. When a failure occurs, collect the exact error message, caller identity, requested action, resource ARN, account, Region, and timestamp. Change only the configuration responsible for the failure, then repeat the operation using the same identity and environment.\n\n### 6. DevOps pipeline application\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, this section should become an explicit and testable pipeline responsibility. The pipeline should validate its prerequisites, use an approved IAM role, execute the required operation, inspect the result, and stop when validation fails. Store the resulting image reference and digest with the build metadata. Pass verified artifact information to the deployment stage so that downstream environments use the intended image rather than an unverified local build.\n\n### 7. Engineering checklist\n\n- Confirm the correct AWS account and Region.\n- Confirm the ECR repository and image reference.\n- Use the intended IAM identity and least-privilege permissions.\n- Verify the operation using AWS metadata or Docker inspection.\n- Record the image tag and digest where applicable.\n- Check security, retention, and rollback requirements.\n- Preserve diagnostic evidence and avoid exposing credentials."
