        "content": "## Production Hands-on Lab — Image Tagging and Digest Management — Production Hands-on Lab [stage-9-topic-17-section-18]\n\n### 1. Technical explanation\n\nExplain how the image reference identifies the artifact, how tags and digests differ, how the value is assigned and verified, and how it affects release traceability. Include the risks of mutable references and the value of immutable release identifiers.\n\nThis production exercise demonstrates a controlled container artifact workflow using ECR. It covers repository readiness, IAM identity, Docker authentication, image publication, digest verification, vulnerability review, runtime retrieval, and rollback preparation.\n\n### 2. How this works in practice\n\nConfirm the AWS account and Region. Build an image from a known source revision, assign a unique release tag, authenticate to ECR, push the image, and record its digest. Review scan findings and deploy the approved digest to a test workload. Verify runtime access and application health.\n\nFor this section, the engineer should understand the relationship between the selected operation and the complete ECR image lifecycle. Confirm the target AWS account, Region, repository, and image reference before performing an operation. Use the identity intended for the actual pipeline or runtime instead of relying only on an administrator account. Record the relevant resource ARN, image tag, digest, and operation result so the work can be traced later.\n\n### 3. Practical AWS and Docker implementation\n\nThe following commands are relevant examples for this topic. Replace ACCOUNT_ID, repository names, image versions, role names, and Regions with values from the actual environment. Review commands before running any operation that changes AWS resources.\n\naws sts get-caller-identity\naws ecr get-login-password --region ap-south-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com\ndocker build -t payments-api:1.4.2 .\ndocker tag payments-api:1.4.2 ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/payments-api:1.4.2\ndocker push ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/payments-api:1.4.2\naws ecr describe-images --repository-name payments-api --region ap-south-1\n\n### 4. Security and production considerations\n\nUse a dedicated CI role, least-privilege repository permissions, immutable release tags, encryption, vulnerability scanning, and audit logging. Perform a negative test using a restricted identity and confirm that unauthorized publishing is denied.\n\nProduction implementation should be repeatable and reviewable. Keep infrastructure configuration and IAM policies in version control. Use short-lived credentials, protect sensitive command output, and separate development and production responsibilities. Record image provenance and verify the exact artifact that is promoted. Do not treat a successful command as sufficient evidence until the resulting AWS resource or image metadata has been checked.\n\n### 5. Troubleshooting and verification\n\nTest a deliberately incorrect repository reference, a restricted IAM identity, and a missing image tag in a non-production environment. Diagnose each failure using caller identity, repository metadata, IAM policies, and runtime events. Never introduce destructive tests into a production repository.\n\nVerify the outcome by checking the AWS CLI response, repository configuration, image metadata, tag, digest, and relevant CloudTrail activity. When a failure occurs, collect the exact error message, caller identity, requested action, resource ARN, account, Region, and timestamp. Change only the configuration responsible for the failure, then repeat the operation using the same identity and environment.\n\n### 6. DevOps pipeline application\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, this section should become an explicit and testable pipeline responsibility. The pipeline should validate its prerequisites, use an approved IAM role, execute the required operation, inspect the result, and stop when validation fails. Store the resulting image reference and digest with the build metadata. Pass verified artifact information to the deployment stage so that downstream environments use the intended image rather than an unverified local build.\n\n### 7. Engineering checklist\n\n- Confirm the correct AWS account and Region.\n- Confirm the ECR repository and image reference.\n- Use the intended IAM identity and least-privilege permissions.\n- Verify the operation using AWS metadata or Docker inspection.\n- Record the image tag and digest where applicable.\n- Check security, retention, and rollback requirements.\n- Preserve diagnostic evidence and avoid exposing credentials."
